diff --git a/docs/spec/decisions.md b/docs/spec/decisions.md
index d24e7a20..72b91a7b 100644
--- a/docs/spec/decisions.md
+++ b/docs/spec/decisions.md
@@ -78,6 +78,7 @@ edits the existing row and notes the supersession (do not delete).
 | D61 | Inline LLM-triggering dispatch (`/retry --digest`) stays outside the M1-636 per-user interruptible-concurrency cap | The D35 non-interruptible inline-dispatch class — `/retry --digest` is its only v1 member — is deliberately NOT counted against (and not checked by) the per-user cross-scope concurrency cap. Bounding rationale: it dispatches inline on the adapter's single-threaded transport dispatch executor, so it can never take a pool worker (the resource the cap protects) and self-serializes to at most one concurrent call per adapter identity; its rate stays metered by the same per-user per-minute LLM bucket as chat replies plus the per-group backstop, with per-group single-flight + cooldown in the digest-retry path, group-admin-only authorization, and a pre-cap audit row. Counting it coherently would require either an asymmetric check (the re-roll checks the sender's count but is never itself counted — incoherent: digest spam alone would stay unbounded by it) or expanding the M1-638 turn-lifecycle registry to lifecycle-less dispatch (no `/stop` window, no worker slot) — real design surface for a residual the 2026-07-17 redteam re-audit rated bounded (worst case +1 concurrent call). **Structural preconditions** — the exclusion is sound only while BOTH hold: (a) inline dispatch runs on the single-threaded transport path (moving digest retry to any pooled/async path voids the self-serialization bound), and (b) every inline LLM surface draws from the per-minute LLM bucket. A ticket adding a second inline LLM-triggering surface, or moving `/retry --digest` off the transport thread, must revisit this row deliberately rather than inherit it. Companion coupling from the same re-audit: `per-user-cap >= max-concurrency` REFUSES BOOT — cap >= pool silently voids the single-sender-cannot-fill-the-pool property (M1-610 enforcement precedent). Operator decisions 2026-07-17. Landed by M1-639. |
 | D62 | Periodic digest structure is deterministic tag arithmetic; the LLM touches only per-cluster prose | The non-degraded periodic group digest groups its clusters under topic category headers computed by **pure tag arithmetic** — a qualifying-tag threshold (a tag must be carried by at least N clusters, default 3), highest-digest-wide-count assignment with alphabetical tie-break, a fold-back second pass that folds under-threshold categories into Other, section order by assigned-cluster count descending then alphabetical with Other always last, a per-section item cap (default 12) with a localized "+N more" line, and one closing affordance line (`commands.md` §Periodic group digests). No LLM call participates in the structure: given the same clusters + tags the section layout is byte-identical, extending the D19 "deterministic retrieval, LLM only for prose" boundary from the retrieved set to the digest's rendered structure. Per-cluster LLM prose is generated only for the clusters actually shown (capped-out clusters would waste LLM calls on never-rendered prose). The per-cluster prose step, `/summary`'s flat interactive format, and the degraded digest (D17) are unchanged. |
 | D64 | Outbound delivery is at-least-once; `correlationId` is not a dedup key and no chokepoint mechanism will make it one | v1 outbound delivery is **at-least-once**: a retry after an ambiguous send failure MAY deliver a duplicate, and nothing suppresses it. `OutboundMessage.correlationId`'s only SPI commitment is **non-nullity** — the prior javadoc claim that it is "stable across retries of the same logical outbound" was **false** and is retired, as is `06-messaging.md` §6.3.5's "adapters SHOULD deduplicate by correlationId over a 60-second window" (no adapter ever did). Stability is a per-call-site property: all but three `new OutboundMessage(...)` sites mint a fresh `UUID.randomUUID()`, so a correlationId-keyed dedup table is inert at nearly every site, and the three stable-id sites (`DigestWorker`, `ApproveGroupCommandHandler`, `RejectGroupCommandHandler`) are deliberate re-sends that must NOT be suppressed — `/retry --digest` is spec-mandated to post again (`commands.md` §Conversation control). The retired 60 s window was also shorter than the sequence it covered: back-off sleeps total under 750 ms, but `SimpleXAdapter.ACK_TIMEOUT` is 30 s per transmit, so three attempts span ~90 s (× N for an N-chunk send). **The bar to reopening this is a transport-level fact, not a Provider-side design:** the duplicate arises from an ambiguous transmit, and only the adapter or transport can observe whether it landed — a Provider chokepoint records success only after the adapter reports it, so it is structurally blind to that case. Any future at-most-once work is therefore adapter/transport-level. This row exists because the false stability claim was load-bearing in two ticket designs (M1-642, M1-652) before anyone checked the call sites; recording the *reason* the guarantee is unavailable is what keeps it from being re-derived. Recorded by M1-653 (pure-doc; no behavior change). |
+| D66 | The command-intent corpus is the second embedded corpus; retrieval is match-not-assert, tier-filter-before-return, and explicitly silent on chat delivery | The `doc_embedding` table (V60) is the second embedded corpus alongside `post_embedding` (V11) — one Postgres, one pgvector, one embedding model, both corpora at the 768 dimension pinned by `embedding_metadata`'s singleton (D54). The shape is the structural opposite of `post_embedding` on every load-bearing axis: NOT partitioned (a command-intent document has no TTL — it is correct until the command itself changes), Provider-written (the `CommandIntentIndexBuilder` runs at Provider startup and is the sole writer), and grant-opened to provider INSERT + DELETE so the DELETE-then-INSERT upsert can run (UPDATE withheld; the narrow-grant posture the M1-648 r2 CLEAN audit verified). Three invariants govern the read path (the `helpLookup` chat tool): (1) **Match-not-assert** — embedded text is used only for MATCHING, never for ASSERTING. The tool returns the matched command NAME plus the catalogue's one-line short-help line, composed at call time from the runtime `HelpCommandHandler.CATALOGUE`'s `bundleKey`; full usage/example bodies never enter the model context (the second out-of-scope entry of M1-664). A stale intent row can degrade a match but can never produce wrong syntax, because the tool never reads the stored text. (2) **Tier-filter-before-return** — the caller's visible command-name set is bound as `target_ref = ANY(?)` INSIDE the WHERE of the pgvector query, so an invisible command's name never enters the model context. This is the M1-647 existence-oracle defense widened to free-text input (a wider attack surface than the slash-command unknown-name path). The visible set is computed by the SAME `HelpCommandHandler.visible` tier predicate `/help` applies at listing time, resolved for the chat-tool identity via `HelpCommandHandler.resolveCallerTier(userId, scopeKind, scopeId)`. (3) **D19 determinism** — the match is decided entirely by SQL (pgvector cosine + threshold + tier filter), reproducible on unchanged DB state; the LLM never picks the match. This row is **explicitly silent on chat delivery**: how a matched command's usage reaches the user is the M1-663 spec amendment, implemented by M1-665. The M1-648 r2 redteam regression (medium INJECTION: post-sanitize, model-elected append of privileged command usage) is what the silence buys — the delivery decision is made deliberately elsewhere, not inherited here. Companion to M1-647 (the probation-reachable synonym path, NOT superseded by this corpus — both tiers ship). Recorded by M1-664. |
 
